--TEST--
Core\IO::isReadable predicts whether ::read succeeds for both a file that is there and a name that is not, while ::isWritable answers `false` for a name ::write then creates without complaint — the divergence is PHP's `is_writable` and is kept on purpose, because the question is about the path as it is rather than about what a write would do with it — spec § 14, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The agreement first, counted rather than read off a line: for each of a name
// that is there and a name that is not, the guard and the operation say the
// same thing. A member that answered plausibly for the present file alone
// still fails this. Each name is joined onto this file's folder, which
// `Here::path` returns.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
string $dir = Here::path('.');
array<string> $paths = ["seed.txt", "missing.txt"];
int $n = Core\Arr::count($paths) as int;

int $agree = 0;
int $i = 0;
for (; $i < $n; $i = $i + 1) {
    string $path = Core\Path::join($dir, $paths[$i]);
    bool $guard = Core\IO::isReadable($path);
    bool $read = true;
    try {
        string $held = Core\IO::read($path);
    } catch (IOError $failed) {
        $read = false;
    }
    if ($guard == $read) {
        $agree = $agree + 1;
    }
}
echo "isReadable agrees with read: ", $agree as string, " of ", $n as string, "\n";

// And the divergence, which is the whole reason this member is not a
// substitute for trying. A name that is not there is not writable -- there is
// no path for a permission to be about -- and `write` creates it anyway,
// because creating is what `write` does.
if (Core\IO::isWritable("fresh.txt")) {
    echo "fresh.txt: writable before it exists\n";
} else {
    echo "fresh.txt: not writable before it exists\n";
}

Core\IO::write("fresh.txt", "made\n");

if (Core\IO::isWritable("fresh.txt")) {
    echo "fresh.txt: writable once it does\n";
} else {
    echo "fresh.txt: not writable once it does\n";
}

// A program asking "may I create a file here" is asking about the directory,
// and that is the path to hand over.
if (Core\IO::isWritable(".")) {
    echo "the directory it was created in: writable\n";
} else {
    echo "the directory it was created in: not writable\n";
}
--FILE seed.txt--
kept
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
isReadable agrees with read: 2 of 2
fresh.txt: not writable before it exists
fresh.txt: writable once it does
the directory it was created in: writable
